Q: Javascript, CSS: Get element by style attribute
A: <p>My suggestion is avoid doing this if at all remotely possible. Instead, use a class to assign the color value, and then you can look up the elements using the class, rather than the color value.</p>

<p>As far as I'm aware, there's no selector (not even in CSS3) that you can use to query a <em>specific</em> style value, which means looping through all elements (or it looks like you can restrict it to all elements with a <code>style</code> attribute) and looking at the <code>element.style.color</code> property. Now, the thing is, even though you write <code>color: #333;</code> in your <code>style</code> attribute, different browsers will echo it back to you in different ways. It might be <code>#333</code>, it might be <code>#333333</code>, it might be <code>rgb(51, 51, 51)</code>, it might even be <code>rgba(51, 51, 51, 0)</code>.</p>

